INT. BRANDVOLD TAXIDERMY - BACK ROOM - NIGHT

Glass eyes glitter from every shelf: owls, a badger, a stuffed marlin mounted over the door. Under a green lamp, a poker game is winding down.

OTTO BRANDVOLD, 60s, round and genial, shuffles with slow pride. Across from him sits SILAS KEANE, 40s, lean, in a good suit gone shiny at the elbows. TOM PELLETIER, 50s, a bruiser in a too-small vest, stacks his chips like bricks. MAUDE ARCHIBALD, 50s, silver hair, pearl earrings, knits between hands. RUBY LAMB, 20s, sharp-eyed, deals herself a cigarette and nothing else.

OTTO
Gentlemen. Ladies. A toast before the last hand. To friends who stay friends.

He pours amber into five small glasses.

TOM
Never turn down Otto's brandy.

MAUDE
(to Ruby)
Dear, you're not drinking.

RUBY
I'm pacing myself.

Silas lifts his glass, studies it against the lamp, sets it down untouched.

SILAS
(muttering)
Nobody pours that generous for free.

OTTO
Something wrong, Silas?

SILAS
Just admiring the craftsmanship. Of the glass.

Tom drains his and thumps the table. Maude's needles click. The marlin's shadow crawls across the felt.

OTTO
Last hand. Everybody in?

ALL
(ragged)
In.

Otto deals. Tom sways, blinks hard, and tries to speak. Nothing comes. He slides sideways off his chair and lands among the sawdust.

Maude's needles stop. Ruby stands, calm, and pockets the cards.

RUBY
(to Silas)
You didn't drink it.

SILAS
Neither did you.

OTTO
(to the room, softly)
Two out of five. Not bad for a Tuesday.

The back door opens. FENWICK, 30s, rain on his shoulders, a badge in his raised hand and a revolver in the other.

FENWICK
Nobody touch the glasses. Otto, I've been waiting a year for this.

Otto smiles and sets down the bottle.

OTTO
So has Maude.

Maude lays her knitting aside. Beneath it, a small pistol.
